 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **No Sign Change:** If the polynomial does not change sign within the cell, there is no intersection [G].
-- **Precision:** Single-precision arithmetic is used, which yields numerically stable results [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Gradient Computation:** Normals are computed using a forward-difference stencil on the same cell to avoid the high cost of neighbor-finding [S3].
+*   **Cell Boundaries:** Numerical issues arise when the ray origin is near the edge of a cell. The method mitigates this by setting the local cell position to the midpoint of the ray's overlap with the cell [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Gradient Artifacts:** When computing normals, clamping in the gradient computation can produce artifacts at cell boundaries. Reducing `delta_step` reduces these artifacts but may make the surface appear bumpier due to less smooth normals [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Sign Changes:** The algorithm relies on detecting sign changes. If the isovalue is exactly equal to a corner value or a critical point, special handling (e.g., epsilon comparison) is required [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The root finding uses iterative bisection, converging logarithmically with precision. Each iteration evaluates the trilinear polynomial in constant time [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** O(1) additional memory per ray, as no cell data needs to be stored per ray [S3].
-- **Performance:** Slightly faster than analytical cubic solvers [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Numerically stable in single precision [S1][S2, unofficial]. Simple to implement.
-- **Limitations:** Iterative nature may be slower than a direct analytical solution if the solver is highly optimized, but the source claims it is faster [S1][S2, unofficial]. Requires cell reconstruction (neighbor finding) which is costly if not cached [S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The polynomial evaluation and bisection are constant time per cell, $O(1)$. The bisection is fixed to a small number of iterations (e.g., 3) [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory per ray-cell intersection.
+*   **Performance:** It is slightly faster than analytical cubic solvers like Schwarze’s method [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Yields single-precision, numerically stable results [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast, stable, suitable for real-time rendering (e.g., WebGPU) [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Approximate due to fixed bisection iterations; may miss roots if the function is highly oscillatory within the cell (though trilinear interpolation is generally well-behaved) [G].
 
 ## Background
-The method is based on the Neubauer iterative root finder proposed by Marmitt et al. [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is described in the context of isosurface ray tracing of octree volumes [S1][S2, unofficial]. The approach was compared to Schwarze’s cubic solver used by Parker et al. and found to be faster and more stable in single precision [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Origin:** Proposed by Marmitt et al. in 2003/2004 [S1][S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. The method is described in "Fast and Accurate Ray-Voxel Intersection Techniques for Iso-Surface Ray Tracing" [S4, unofficial].
+*   **History:** It was used in the context of interactive isosurface ray tracing of large octree volumes [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is often compared to Parker et al.'s use of Schwarze’s cubic solver [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Used in WebGPU implicit isosurface raycasters [S4, unofficial].
 
